message:
I am thinking...: Let me understand the vulnerability. The issue is a heap-use-after-free in MuPDF's PDF object resolution path (pdf_cache_object -> pdf_resolve_indirect -> pdf_resolve_indirect_chain).

Looking at pdf_cache_object, the key area is the 'o' type handling (object stream). Let me look at the relevant code more carefully:

```c
else if (x->type == 'o')
{
    if (!x->obj)
    {
        pdf_xref_entry *orig_x = x;
        orig_x->type = 'O'; /* Mark this node so we know we're recursing. */
        fz_try(ctx)
            x = pdf_load_obj_stm(ctx, doc, x->ofs, &doc->lexbuf.base, num);
        fz_always(ctx)
            orig_x->type = 'o'; /* Not recursing any more. */
        fz_catch(ctx)
            fz_rethrow(ctx);
        if (x == NULL)
            fz_throw(...);
        if (!x->obj)
        {
            x->type = 'f';
            orig_x->type = 'f';
            ...
        }
    }
}
```

The key issue: `pdf_load_obj_stm` may reallocate the xref table (because loading an object stream may trigger repair or xref growth), which would invalidate the `orig_x` pointer. After `pdf_load_obj_stm` returns, `orig_x` points to freed memory (if the xref table was reallocated). Then `orig_x->type = 'o'` writes to freed memory -> heap-use-after-free.

Actually, let me look at the actual oss-fuzz issue. The bug is in pdf_cache_object. Let me look at the git history / the actual fix to understand precisely.

Let me look at the fuzz harness to understand what input format is expected.

